LEARNING_PROTOCOL_VERSION 0.1.0

STATUS DRAFT

OWNER President Cley Duarte

DEPENDS_ON

ontology/PACE_ONTOLOGY_0.1.0.pdl (REQUEST entity, PROMOTED_FROM relation,
  HAS_CONTINUITY_NOTE assertion)
governance/GOVERNANCE_PROTOCOL_0.1.0.pdl (authority: PACE agents advise
  and audit, they never override the ROOT_AUTHORITY)

PURPOSE

Define how PACE learns from a correction once and never forces the same
question again. Every permanent observation from the ROOT_AUTHORITY enters
as a REQUEST, is classified by SCOPE, and — once approved — is promoted
into a binding rule that every future AI loads automatically at handoff.
This is the mechanism behind Vision point 6: an observation becoming a
policy, a protocol, a standard, or a rule of behaviour for AIs.

PROBLEM

An AI that re-asks a question already answered wastes the ROOT_AUTHORITY's
time and proves the knowledge was never captured. A correction that lives
only in one chat is lost the moment the session, the AI, or the month
changes. PACE must turn each correction into a durable, machine-loaded
rule so it is answered exactly once, ever.

INTAKE

Every correction, preference or lesson is logged as a REQUEST the moment
it is stated — before it is acted on. Core fields (WHO, DATE, RAW_TEXT)
are immutable once logged. No correction is binding merely by having been
said (Governance rule 5); it becomes binding only when promoted.

SCOPE

Every REQUEST, and every rule promoted from it, carries a mandatory SCOPE
— one of PACE's three levels of authority:

  PACE          A lesson about the engine itself — the PDL format, the
                Kernel, the CLI, or how any project is governed. Lives in
                the PACE repository (policies/, protocols/, standards/)
                and applies to every governed project.

  ORGANIZATION  A preference of authority or way of working that applies
                across all of the ROOT_AUTHORITY's projects. Lives in the
                organization's own governed-unit instance.

  PROJECT       A lesson true only of one product — its domain,
                architecture or conventions. Lives in that project's
                .pace/ (decisions/ or memory/persistent/) and applies to
                it alone.

CLASSIFICATION

The AI never fixes SCOPE on its own. It PROPOSES a SCOPE using this
heuristic, and the ROOT_AUTHORITY confirms or overrides it at approval:

  - speaks of PDL, the Kernel, the CLI, or how projects are governed
    -> PROPOSE PACE
  - speaks of the domain, architecture or conventions of one specific
    product -> PROPOSE PROJECT
  - a standing preference of authority or style that applies to
    everything -> PROPOSE ORGANIZATION

When genuinely ambiguous, the AI states both candidates and asks once —
that single question is then settled forever by the resulting rule.

PROMOTION

On approval, a REQUEST changes STATUS to PROMOTED and gains PROMOTED_TO,
pointing at the artifact it became: a POLICY, PROTOCOL, STANDARD, or a
rule of behaviour for AIs, at its declared SCOPE. History is not
rewritten: the original REQUEST is kept, and the new artifact cites it
via PROMOTED_FROM. A REJECTED or DEFERRED request keeps its RAW_TEXT so
the same idea is not re-litigated from scratch.

HANDOFF_LOADING

This is what stops the re-asking. At the start of every session, the
Bootstrap/Handoff engine loads all APPROVED rules in force for the
current unit — PACE-level rules always, plus the active project's own
PROJECT-level rules, plus the ORGANIZATION-level rules above it — and the
AI obeys them without asking. A question is only ever put to the
ROOT_AUTHORITY for something no approved rule already covers.

GUARANTEE

Answered once, binding forever, loaded automatically. The ROOT_AUTHORITY
decides each rule exactly one time; no AI, present or future, re-asks a
question an approved rule already settles.

END
